 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Camera Intrinsic Matrix Construction](3112-camera-intrinsic-matrix-construction.md)
-Constructs the 3x3 camera intrinsic matrix $K$ from focal length and image dimensions, serving as the bridge between pixel coordinates and normalized device coordinates (NDC) for ray generation. It differs from [Pinhole Camera Ray Generation](5058-pinhole-camera-ray-generation.md) by explicitly building the calibration matrix $K$ rather than assuming a canonical pinhole model with unit focal length and center at the image center. Choose it when precise calibration parameters are available.
+Constructs the $3 \times 3$ intrinsic matrix $K$ from focal length and image dimensions. It assumes zero skew and an aspect ratio of 1 (or incorporates aspect ratio into the focal length scaling).
+*   **Formula:** $K = \begin{bmatrix} f_{\text{camera}} & 0 & W/2 \\ 0 & f_{\text{camera}} & H/2 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}$.
+*   **Parameters:** $f_{\text{camera}}$ is the focal length in pixels, $W$ is image width, $H$ is image height.
+*   **When to choose:** Use when the camera is calibrated with a single focal length parameter or when simplifying the intrinsic model for synthetic data generation.
 
 ### [Pinhole Camera Ray Generation](5058-pinhole-camera-ray-generation.md)
-Computes the origin and direction vectors for rays corresponding to each pixel on an image plane, assuming an ideal pinhole camera model with no lens distortion or depth of field. It explicitly constructs the view frustum geometry (basis vectors and view plane bounds) from high-level camera parameters (eye position, look-at point, up vector, and field of view) rather than relying on a pre-computed projection matrix. Choose it for simple, parameter-driven camera setups without explicit calibration matrices.
-
-### Camera Intrinsics Simplification
-Constructs the 3x3 camera intrinsic matrix $K$ assuming zero skew and an aspect ratio of 1 [S31, unofficial]. The matrix is defined as $K = \begin{bmatrix} f_{\text{camera}} & 0 & W/2 \\ 0 & f_{\text{camera}} & H/2 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}$, where $f_{\text{camera}}$ is the focal length, $W$ is image width, and $H$ is image height [S31, unofficial]. This simplification assumes square pixels and a principal point at the image center [S31, unofficial].
+Computes ray origins and directions assuming an ideal pinhole model where all rays pass through a single center point. Unlike Camera Matrix Ray Generation, which uses explicit $K$ and $c2w$ matrices, this method often derives rays directly from geometric primitives such as field of view (FOV), aspect ratio, and camera basis vectors (look-at formulation). It provides no depth of field.
+*   **When to choose:** Use for simple synthetic scenes or when camera parameters are defined by FOV and position rather than calibration matrices.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ to generate rays for all pixels [G]. The operations are element-wise vector math, highly parallelizable on GPU [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ to store the origin and direction tensors [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Explicit control over camera calibration; supports arbitrary principal points and focal lengths; standard in NeRF and modern rendering pipelines [S11, S17, S27, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate calibration data; sensitive to errors in $K$ or $T_{c2w}$; division by zero if focal length is 0 [S15, S20, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When camera parameters are known from calibration (e.g., COLMAP, sensor data) or when precise control over projection geometry is needed [S11, S17, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** For simple orthographic views or when only relative camera motion is available without scale/intrinsics [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(H \times W)$ for generating rays for all pixels. The operations are vectorizable and highly parallelizable on GPU [S12, S20, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(H \times W)$ to store the origin and direction tensors [S11, S12, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Highly efficient on modern GPUs due to regular memory access patterns and simple arithmetic operations [S12, S20, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Explicitly handles arbitrary camera poses and intrinsics; robust to complex camera setups (e.g., non-square pixels, principal point offset) [S12, S21, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-computed or calibrated intrinsic and extrinsic matrices; less intuitive for artists defining cameras by FOV and look-at vectors compared to geometric methods [S1, S9, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When working with real-world camera data (e.g., COLMAP, NeRF datasets) where intrinsics and poses are known [S11, S12, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** For simple synthetic scenes where a basic FOV-based setup is sufficient and calibration data is unavailable [S9, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from standard computer graphics and photogrammetry, this method formalizes the pinhole camera model using matrix algebra [G]. It is widely used in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [S11, S17, S27, unofficial] and volume rendering pipelines [S1, S7, unofficial] to generate rays from camera parameters for consistent viewing geometry. The explicit use of the intrinsic matrix $K$ allows for handling non-square pixels and off-center principal points, generalizing the basic pinhole model [S31, unofficial].
+The method originates from standard computer vision and graphics literature on camera models, specifically the pinhole camera model defined by intrinsic and extrinsic parameters [S12, S32, unofficial]. It is widely used in NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) implementations and volume rendering pipelines that ingest real-world camera poses [S9, S11, S12, unofficial]. The use of explicit matrices allows for seamless integration with photogrammetry pipelines (e.g., COLMAP) [S11, S19, unofficial].
 
